# EL-2 — the client's two 0x01 OPEN frames, for message-type coverage.
#
# Provenance: same capture and same session as `live-proxy-login-in.hex`; see
# that file's header. Cited by SHA-256, never by path.
#
# This is our own half, checked in because the frame type is under test and
# there is nothing else to see it in: OPEN is only ever sent client->proxy, so
# a peer-half-only rule would leave type 0x01 with no fixture at all.
#
# It is safe to check in because an OPEN frame is a header and nothing else —
# 9 bytes, zero-length payload. It carries no credential. Its peer IPv4 field
# holds EchoLink's own directory server, which is public infrastructure every
# client is configured with, not an address belonging to another operator.
#
# What it pins: a zero-length payload is legal, and the length field is
# `00000000` rather than the frame being truncated to 5 bytes. A decoder that
# treats "no payload" as "no frame" loses the session lifecycle.
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py <capture> --transport tcp --stream 1 --dir out --range 2:2,7:7
# Capture SHA-256: c66d11f9bce26158c8723c13c2846e62a2a35e040b929a16545f3f8433359ddf
#
# Each line is one EchoLink proxy frame verbatim, reassembled from the
# TCP stream: the 9-byte header — type(1), peer IPv4(4), length(4,
# LITTLE-endian) — and its payload. `[n]` is the frame's index in
# arrival order across both directions, so a gap in the indices is a
# frame deliberately left out.

# [2] 1.010s ==> OPEN        peer 152.67.98.197     0B
01 984362c5 00000000
# [7] 2.529s ==> OPEN        peer 152.67.98.197     0B
01 984362c5 00000000
